{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791192502,"nonce":"ZzxzEykGm3oEX9ZQd90lJReOIDY=","title":"Map\u003cK,V\u003e field in a generic class is stored as an interface fat-pointer copy","message":"With the Issue6833 class forced to run its real constructor:\n\n    private class KeyValueIterator\u003cK,V\u003e {\n        var map:Map\u003cK,V\u003e;\n        var keys:Iterator\u003cK\u003e;\n        public inline function new(map:Map\u003cK,V\u003e) { this.map = map; this.keys = map.keys(); }\n    }\n\n`new KeyValueIterator([1 =\u003e 'hello'])` passes a raw IntMap pointer (haxe_intmap_new). The ctor MIR treats the parameter as an IMap interface fat pointer: it allocates 88 bytes and copies 11 words out of the IntMap, then calls `keys` through `call_indirect` on word 5 of the argument. SIGSEGV in unit.issues.KeyValueIterator.new.\n\nMap\u003cK,V\u003e with unbound K,V should resolve to the same representation the caller passes (or the caller must build the fat pointer). Blocked behind the shadowing issue: until `new` binds to the local class, the ctor never runs.","files":null}]}